// WQL — `avg` OVER A `str` COLUMN IS REFUSED AT THE FRONT END.
//
// THE HOLE THIS CLOSES. `check_agg_operand` — the check that asks whether an
// aggregate's argument reaches its accumulator — was SKIPPED whenever
// `agg_cast` was set, and `agg_cast` was keyed on the argument being NOT FLOAT
// rather than on its being NUMERIC. `avg` over ANY non-float column therefore
// went unchecked, `str` included.
//
// MEASURED at the parent commit (0fb56500), verbatim, on exactly this program:
//   logosc rc = 0
//   mlir_gen: unsupported cast
//   logosc: wrote <obj>            ← 75360 bytes ON DISK
// — a compiler that printed an error, returned success and emitted an object
// file. Two defects in one line: the front end admitted a query it cannot
// compile, and the backend's report did not reach the exit code. This file pins
// the FIRST; `mlir_gen_exit_code_fail` pins the second, and it needs its own
// program precisely because this one no longer reaches mlir-gen at all.
//
// ⚠ THE ROOT WAS THE LAUNDERING, NOT THE EXEMPTION. `compute_agg_reprs` used to
// compute `let aty = if arg_is_flt { FLT } else { INT }` and hand THAT to the
// shared rule table — so the table was asked a question whose answer had already
// been decided, and could never return -1 on the static side for any argument
// type whatever. Deleting the exemption alone would have refused `avg` over a
// `bool` column too, which the ruling admits. The class fix is to stop
// fabricating the class: both tiers now ask the same table the same question.
//
// ⚠ THE REFUSED POPULATION IS CLOSED **IN THE EL LATTICE**, AND IT IS THIS.
// After the ruling admits `bool`, the lattice's only remaining non-numeric class
// is `EL_TY_STR`, whose members are exactly `str` and `String` (el.logos rows 17
// and 18). This file is `str`; `wql_agg_avg_string_arg_fail` is `String`.
//
// ⚠⚠ IT IS NOT CLOSED OVER ARGUMENT TYPES, and an earlier version of this
// comment said "there is no third" without that qualifier. `el_ty_of_name`
// launders every unknown type name to `EL_TY_INT` — the same laundering this
// commit removed one level down — so a STRUCT-typed column reaches the rule
// table spelled INT, `agg_cast` comes out true, and `check_agg_operand` is
// skipped exactly there. MEASURED: `struct Inner` with `avg(s.v)` gives
// `<metaprog-blob-subst>: error [fn means_run]: cannot cast 'Inner' to 'f64'`,
// rc=1, no object written — so it is refused, loudly and safely, but by the HOST
// COMPILER inside a generated blob rather than by the front end naming the
// clause, the aggregate and the column type. That is the half of the ruling this
// file cannot claim. Pinned as current behaviour by wql_agg_avg_struct_arg_fail.
package test;

use logos.std.wql.wql;
use logos.mem.collections.vec;

struct Rec { pub k: i64, pub v: str }

pub deem means(src: &[Rec]) { from src s
      group by s.k
      aggregate a = avg(s.v)
      select a : f64 }

fn main() -> i32 { return 0i32; }
